# AN ALPINE MICROVM DRAWN ON THE VGA ADAPTER: A TEXT CONSOLE, RECORDED.
#
# THIS FILE WAS CALLED `desktop.walk` AND IT HAS NEVER STARTED A DESKTOP. MEASURED on 2026-11-18 by
# taking the mp4 apart frame by frame: every frame after boot is
#
#	Welcome to Alpine Linux 3.21
#	Kernel 6.12.117-0-virt on an x86_64 (/dev/tty1)
#	(none) login:
#
# - a TEXT CONSOLE on the VGA adapter. `openbox` and `xorg-server` were installed in that image and
# were never executed, so the recording was a picture of a login prompt wearing the name of a
# desktop. The name was the bug; the recording was, and is, a real thing to prove.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE ACTUALLY PROVES, AND IT IS NOT NOTHING:
#
#	`console=tty0` makes the kernel mirror its output to the VGA adapter, so the guest DRAWS, and
#	`see:` / `screendump` can photograph it. The mp4 is a picture of the guest rather than a black
#	field - which is what the diskless netboot guest in `verify.walk` produces, because it draws
#	nothing on the adapter at all.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE, AND WHY A DESKTOP CANNOT GO HERE:
#
#	A microvm has no firmware and no PCIe, and a desktop needs one of the two. MEASURED on both
#	adapters this machine type can create:
#
#	  virtio-gpu-device   the device attaches, `virtio_gpu` loads, and its probe fails - the virtio
#	                      status comes back as 0x83 (NEEDS_RESET) and no /dev/dri/card0 ever
#	                      appears, so X answers `(EE) no screens found`
#	  isa-vga             a text-mode adapter: no DRM node, and no /dev/fb0 either, because
#	                      CONFIG_FB_VESA needs a VBE BIOS call and there is no firmware
#
#	`machine.Boot.Validate` now refuses `microvm_display: desktop` by name rather than recording a
#	black field. A desktop is a q35 shape.

walkthrough "alpine microvm - a text console drawn on the VGA adapter, recorded"
  resolution 1024x768
  # THE VIDEO IS THE ARTIFACT, so its size is stated rather than inherited.
  recording_resolution 1024x768

  needs alpine-vmlinuz alpine-initramfs alpine.qcow2

  # THE ROOT FILESYSTEM, BUILT BY `.github/alpine-rootfs.sh` AND NOT INSTALLED BY THIS FILE.
  disk alpine.qcow2

  # `tty0` IS THE WHOLE POINT OF THIS FILE. `ttyS0` alone gives a guest that boots perfectly and a
  # recording that is black, because the kernel only writes to the serial line and never touches the
  # adapter. `with a console` asks for `-device isa-vga`, the text adapter a microvm can create.
  boot as a microvm from kernel "alpine-vmlinuz" and initramfs "alpine-initramfs" with "console=tty0 console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4" with a console with memory 768, waiting up to 180s

  # THE FIRST FACT: init finished and a getty is asking for a login. It is printed by Alpine's own
  # getty, which lives in the ROOT FILESYSTEM, so it cannot appear unless /dev/vda was mounted.
  the console will say "login"

  # TWENTY SECONDS OF THAT PROMPT ON THE SCREEN, so the mp4 is a machine that stayed up rather than
  # a boot log that scrolled past.
  wait 20s

  stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s
